[# Section 19 — XAP feature distribution (the v0.13.0 headline; version-literal-ok). What a
   feature is, authoring, publishing to a registry, consuming by pin,
   entitlements, and the honest marketplace status. Spec home: the
   feature distribution & market spec
   (spec/03-approved/xap/xap_feature_distribution_market.md). The
   how-to developer set that used to sit under docs/dev/
   (features-*, registry-*, marketplace, client-and-views, the ORIEL and
   studio guides) folded into this section under DOCS-46, re-voiced, each
   claim beside a corpus case this guide's cx replays ([fixture id=]) or
   the spec page that holds it. Every command and output on this page was run
   against the branch-head binary — including the pkg pipeline against
   the live external reference registry (issue #425 coverage gap: XAP feature
   distribution). #]

[section id=xap-distribution group='The bindings and ecosystem groups' n=19 title='XAP feature distribution'

  [intro
    """The headline of the 0.13 release is that XAP features became a
       **distribution unit**: sealed, signed, published, discovered,
       verified, installed, and entitled — end to end, on machinery the
       platform already had. Distribution introduces no archive format,
       no transport, and no trust primitive of its own: a package is a
       sealed subtree in a content-addressed store ([[store]]), a
       registry is a store, a version is an alias, and trust is the
       Tier-1 content hash plus a publisher-DID signature. The
       normative model is the feature distribution & market spec
       (`spec/03-approved/xap/xap_feature_distribution_market.md`).
       This section walks the staircase — the unit, authoring,
       publishing, consuming, entitlements, the market — then the
       clients that materialize a surface and the reference surfaces
       built the declarative way, and it is explicit about which steps
       are shipped and which are specified-but-not-yet-built. Running a
       XAP is [[ops-xap-host]]."""]

  [child id=dist-what n=19.1 title='The unit — what a feature is'
    [body
      """A **feature** is the XAP composition unit and the platform's
         commercial unit — the thing you author, version, sell, and
         install. It is a grammar (verbs, nouns, rules — compiled from
         requirements) plus the implementation module that travels with
         it; behavior ships **inside the package**, never in the
         serving process. Not everything is a feature, though — the
         sealing and signing machinery is content-agnostic, and the
         package `kind` decides only which check runs at install:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'kind'] [th 'unit'] [th 'install gate']]
      [tr [td 'feature'] [td 'the XAP composition unit: grammar + impl + fixtures'] [td 'verify + compose-gate + needs consent']]
      [tr [td 'library'] [td 'plain CX code — no grammar, no governance surface'] [td 'verify + module-surface check (declared defs present, Tier-2 identities match)']]
      [tr [td 'client'] [td 'a medium materializer app (a separate project)'] [td 'verify + client-spec validation']]]
    [body
      """Two dependency planes, never blurred: on the **grammar plane**
         a feature `uses` a feature (composition — changes what a
         principal can *say*); on the **code plane** any package
         `requires` a library (implementation, hash-pinned — changes
         what the code *calls*). A library never `uses` a feature, and
         by invariant N-DIST-2 a library carries **no authority**: it
         executes under the requiring feature's grants and can neither
         hold nor request capabilities of its own. Enabled features
         compose into one grammar with conflicts caught as values at
         compose time, and the composed grammar hashes
         deterministically — two deployments with the same pins have
         bit-identical grammars:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?let [= $chart
         [feature name=chart
           [nouns [noun name=viewport singular=true [field name=center type=geo-point]]]
           [verbs [verb name=highlight effect=arrange [intent [do :highlight]] [reads viewport]]]
           [requirements [requirement kind=functional as=user traces=highlight
             [want 'to mark a spot'] [so 'I can find it']]]]]
      [= $strikes
         [feature name=strikes
           [nouns [noun name=strike [field name=pos type=geo-point] [field name=at type=instant]]]
           [verbs [verb name=list-strikes effect=observe [intent [do :list-strikes]] [reads strike]]]
           [requirements [requirement kind=functional as=user traces=list-strikes
             [want 'to see strikes'] [so 'I avoid them']]]]]
      [= $g [$xap:compose $chart $strikes]]
  ([$count $g//verb], [$xap:resolve $g 'highlight' {}], [$xap:grammar-hash $g])]"""]
    [output
      """(2, 'chart/highlight', 'sha2-256:9e0e827745381eda74a7f5ad72174d6f8ab6c384f281dcd0d5d44cd802580d46')"""]
    [note
      """The whole distribution staircase is a **function surface** —
         `[$xap:compose]`, `[$xap:pkg-tree]` / `pkg-seal` / `pkg-sign` /
         `pkg-publish` / `pkg-fetch` / `pkg-verify` / `pkg-install` /
         `pkg-requires-closure` / `pkg-catalog`, and
         `[$xap:license-issue]` / `license-verify` — all shipped in the
         bundled `cx-xap` module (no install). A `cx pkg` CLI verb
         family (e.g. `cx pkg graph`) is named in the spec but not in
         the shipped binary yet; drive the staircase from CX programs
         today."""]]

  [child id=dist-authoring n=19.2 title='Authoring — spec plus contract module'
    [body
      """A feature is authored as a `*.feature.cxd` document validated
         by a schema — requirements are the source, the grammar is the
         compiled output, and a requirement's acceptance criteria are
         its conformance fixtures. The skeleton (the full-size real
         reference is the `reference/shop` app's base feature package):"""]
    [; check=none — a *.feature.cxd is a DATA document consumed by the
       feature toolchain (cx validate --schema=feature.cxs), not a
       runnable program: its intent-template $placeholders ($text) are
       tokens the DATA reading keeps verbatim, but the snippet gate's
       program run would treat them as unbound bindings feeding an
       attribute (CXER0100 under the §6.4.1 scalar-only attr rule). ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[feature name=notes version='0.1.0'
  [summary 'A shared notebook']
  [nouns [noun name=note [field name=text type=text]]]
  [verbs
    [verb name=list-notes effect=observe scope=local consequence=none
      [intent [do :list-notes]] [reads note]]
    [verb name=add-note effect=act scope=shared consequence=reversible
      [intent [do :add-note text=$text]] [writes note]]]
  [governance [grant verb=add-note to=member]]
  [requirements
    [requirement kind=functional as=member traces=add-note
      [want 'to jot a note'] [so 'the crew sees it']]]]"""]
    [body
      """Every verb carries an **effect signature** — `effect`
         (observe · act · arrange) × `scope` × `consequence` — which is
         what the runtime gates scale on: observe is ungated, act meets
         the authorization check, and consequence scales the gate.
         Validation is real tooling, run as you author (the notes
         feature above validates clean against the shipped schema):"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx validate notes.feature.cxd --schema=spec/03-approved/xap/xap_schemas/feature.cxs"""]
    [body
      """The behavior half is the **contract module** — a plain CX
         module exporting the conventional surface the deployment host
         calls: `readout` (always; serves the feature's surface data),
         `apply` (iff the grammar has a non-observe verb; called after
         the policy check admits the intent), and optionally
         `simulate`. A minimal complete module:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?def readout scope=public impure ($host $t)
  [readout title='Notes' tenant=$host@tenant [kv k='count' v='0']]]
[?def apply scope=public impure ($verb $intent $host)
  [?if [= $verb 'notes/add-note']
    [then $verb]
    [else [refused reason='no apply for this verb']]]]"""]
    [body
      """`$host` is the **deployment context** — one value naming the
         deployment the feature runs in:
         `[host tenant='acme' [store <handle>] [journal <handle>
         stream='acts']]` (#1210). `$host@tenant` is the tenant a feature
         acts under (never re-stated from an environment variable),
         `[$first $host/store]` the capability-scoped working store, and
         `[$first $host/journal]` the deployment's act chain — the same
         journal the runtime commits to, so a feature owns its own fold
         instead of opening one. The journal child is ABSENT when the
         deployment declares no journal binding: guard with
         `[$present $host/journal]` (the presence test — `$exists` asks
         content arity)."""]
    [body
      """`$intent` is the committed act itself — `[do 'notes/add-note'
         [text '…']]`, the very value the journal appended — so a field
         reads as a child, `$intent/text`, never through a re-shaped copy
         (one act form at every boundary, #1260). `apply` may refuse on
         domain policy by returning
         `[refused reason=…]` — the policy layer admitted the *verb*,
         the feature refused the *values*; two layers, both visible.
         `scope=public` is what a module exports, and the package
         manifest's `exports` block must list exactly that contract
         surface — projected from the code at publish and re-verified
         at install, both ways."""]
    [body
      """**The filename is part of the contract, not tidiness.** The
         module MUST be at `<feature-name>.cx` — the name the
         `[feature name=…]` carries, exactly. Three readers resolve the
         entry point by that path and nothing else: `pkg:` module
         references, the install gate, and the host's own acquisition;
         a package may carry other `.cx` entries (a vendored helper), so
         "whichever `.cx` is in the tree" is not a rule anything could
         apply. `pkg-seal`, `pkg-install` and host boot each refuse a
         mismatch with `CXER4884` naming the missing entry (#1195) —
         before that enforcement a mismatched package sealed, published,
         verified, composed and booted, and then served an empty
         readout for the life of the deployment. The exported surface
         is checked both ways: code with no `exports` block is refused,
         and a declared export the code does not define is refused
         (the distribution spec's feature runtime contract section)."""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'export'] [th 'required'] [th 'called']]
      [tr [td 'readout ($host $t)'] [td 'always'] [td 'serves GET /surface/<feature>; feeds the push channel']]
      [tr [td 'apply ($verb $intent $host)'] [td 'iff the grammar has a non-observe verb'] [td 'after the policy check admits the emit; $verb arrives qualified; $intent is the committed act itself']]
      [tr [td 'simulate ($host $t $params)'] [td 'optional'] [td 'the host\'s simulation tick']]]
    [body
      """Three authoring rules that hold a feature set together.
         **Requirement and verb trace each other** — every verb traces
         to a requirement and every requirement to a verb, enforced as
         a validation concern, and a requirement's acceptance criteria
         are its conformance fixtures. **Frames and keys** relate nouns
         *across* features: a frame is a shared continuous coordinate
         (compare by nearness — geo, time, value), a key a shared
         identifier (join by sameness); register onto them, never
         invent private ones for a shared concept. **The source stack
         is a seam**: gateways (`kind` deliberately not enumerated) plus
         prioritised sensors with failover feed the nouns, and the noun
         shapes are the stable contract — nothing above the stack
         changes when a vendor does. Your verbs already project a form:
         `[$ux:form $feature 'register' {}]` derives it from the verb's
         declared parameters, falling back to the fields of the noun it
         writes, and the same entry takes a composed grammar
         ([[ux-capability]]):"""]
    [fixture id=ux-080-feature-form-projects-the-xap-grammar]
    [body
      """A **composite feature** is just a feature that `uses` others
         and declares derived nouns (a `[from …]` join over shared
         frames and keys). Composition is closed under feature: the
         composite packages, publishes and installs like a base
         feature, with its `uses` set as grammar-plane dependencies,
         and its cross-feature reads are exactly what the `needs`
         block's `reads` consent is for ([[dist-entitlements]]). The
         full-size reference is the XAP repository's `reference/shop`:
         its base features, and its composite delayed-shipment feature
         joining two of them over geo and time into derived alert
         nouns ([[dist-reference-surfaces]])."""]]

  [child id=dist-registry n=19.3 title='Publishing — a git repo is a registry'
    [body
      """A registry is just a CX store holding sealed packages,
         manifests, and an alias index — so a git repository containing
         a `file://` store **is a complete registry**, with
         publish-by-PR, history as audit, and repo permissions as
         publish permissions. The publish pipeline is
         `author → validate → seal → sign → publish`, and the whole
         publisher side runs against any store:"""]
    [; check=none — the pipeline needs --allow-random for the
       publisher keypair; under the grant-free docs-example gate the
       denial cascades into non-0271 pkg errors (CXER4880/4886). Run
       verified live 2026-07-15 with --allow-random; output pinned
       below. ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/crypto' :as crypto]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/did' :as did]
[?let [= $reg [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $kp [$crypto:ed25519-keypair]]
      [= $pub [$did:key-create $kp@public]]
      [= $tree [$xap:pkg-tree ([entry path='geo-utils.cx' '[?def id scope=public ($x) $x]'])]]
      [= $draft [?element 'package'
                  [?attr 'name' 'geo-utils'] [?attr 'version' '0.1.0'] [?attr 'kind' 'library']
                  [?element 'publisher' [?attr 'did' $pub]]
                  [?element 'exports' [?element 'def' [?attr 'name' 'geo-utils/id']]]]]
      [= $sealed [$xap:pkg-seal $reg $tree $draft]]
      [= $signed [$xap:pkg-sign [$store:get-doc $reg $sealed@manifest] $kp@private]]
      [= $mh [$store:put-doc $reg $signed]]
      [= $pubr [$xap:pkg-publish $reg 'geo-utils' '0.1.0' $mh]]
      [= $v [$xap:pkg-verify $reg 'geo-utils@0.1.0']]
  ($pubr@alias, $v@status, $v@kind)]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-random publish.cx
('geo-utils@0.1.0', 'ok', 'library')"""]
    [list
      [item """**pkg-tree** seals the directory as one canonical
              content document — entries sorted by path, byte-stable, so
              its store hash *is* the package's Tier-1 identity
              (traversal and duplicate paths are rejected,
              `CXER4880`)."""]
      [item """**pkg-seal** validates the manifest draft kind-aware (a
              `needs` block on a library is rejected — libraries carry
              no authority) and stores the manifest beside the tree."""]
      [item """**pkg-sign** fills the detached ed25519 signature over
              the Tier-1 hash. Signing is possession; verification is
              trust — the key-to-DID binding is checked at verify
              time."""]
      [item """**pkg-publish** points the `name@version` alias at the
              manifest. **Released aliases are immutable**: re-pointing
              raises `CXER4887`; re-publishing the identical hash is
              idempotent."""]
      [item """**pkg-verify** re-runs the full fail-closed chain
              (re-hash, signature, required credentials); nothing is staged
              on failure."""]]
    [body
      """Sign from day one, even internally — it costs nothing, and it
         makes every later growth stage a **re-host, never a
         re-package**: the live instance was the original external reference registry
         (stage 1, sealed+signed packages in a git repo), the same
         store served over the store daemon is stage 2 (consumers
         switch the URI scheme, nothing else), and the market of the
         last subsection wraps stage 2. Hashes and signatures never
         change across stages, and a re-hosted package keeps its
         original publisher's signature — trust is per-package, never
         per-registry. The sealed tree is canonical — entries sorted by
         path, byte-stable — which is what makes its hash the package's
         identity:"""]
    [fixture id=xap-dist-001-tree-canonical-order]
    [body
      """A stage-1 registry is a directory in a git repository:
         `registry/keys/<publisher>.cxd` (the publisher identity — a
         `did:key` plus its signing seed, committed by design at stage
         1, because repository permission IS publish permission),
         `registry/store/` (the `file://` CX store holding the sealed
         packages, the manifests and the alias index), and the three
         programs that drive it — `publish.cx`, `pin.cx`, `rehost.cx`.
         Consumers reach it as `file://` (a clone or a vendored copy)
         or `https://` (raw); git adds exactly what an internal registry
         wants — publish by pull request (moving an alias is a reviewed
         commit), history as audit, repository permissions as publish
         permissions."""]
    [body
      """**Manifests: hand-written versus projected.** The draft
         `package.cxd` carries only what cannot be derived — `kind`,
         `publisher`, `requires` (the code plane), `compatibility`,
         `license-terms`, and the `exports` contract surface. The
         grammar summary, the `needs` consent set and the `uses`
         dependencies are projected from the feature spec at publish
         and re-verified at install, never restated by hand; the
         consuming project's `registry/publish.cx` is that projection,
         driven by its make targets (`registry-publish`,
         `registry-publish-all`, `registry-publish-lib`,
         `registry-rehost-<name>`). **Keys**: a stage-1 key is an
         ed25519 seed, the DID a `did:key` — offline-verifiable, no
         resolver infrastructure; rotation and compromise recovery are
         DID-document rotation per the did module spec, with
         attestations re-anchoring trust for pre-rotation artifacts.
         The growth path, each stage a re-host of the same bytes:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'stage'] [th 'what'] [th 'status']]
      [tr [td '0 — in-repo'] [td 'directories and path references'] [td 'the floor']]
      [tr [td '1 — an internal git registry'] [td 'sealed, signed packages, publish by pull request'] [td 'implemented and live (the original external reference instance; the in-family successor is the XAP repository\'s reference/shop)']]
      [tr [td '2 — a served registry'] [td 'the same store served over the store wire by cx store-serve; consumers switch the URL scheme'] [td 'implemented (the wire re-host is engine-tested; pkg-catalog works locally and served)']]
      [tr [td '3 — a market'] [td 'the market XAP wraps the registry'] [td 'the model specified, the entitlement machinery shipped — [[dist-market]]']]]]

  [child id=dist-consuming n=19.4 title='Consuming — verify, install, pin'
    [body
      """The consumer pipeline is `discover → acquire → verify →
         gate → consent → enable`, fail-closed at every step, and
         every check is offline-verifiable — a vendored mirror is as
         good as the origin. Discovery and verification against a
         registry store — this exact program, pointed at the
         repository's own registry (`registry/store`, a git-hosted
         `file://` store whose alias table `[$store:list-aliases]`
         lists `gtin@0.1.0`), was run live for this page:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $reg [$store:open-opts 'file://registry/store'
                                [map read-only='true']]]
      [= $v [$xap:pkg-verify $reg 'gtin@0.1.0']]
      [= $cat [$xap:pkg-catalog $reg {term: 'gtin'}]]
  ($v@status, $v@kind, [$count $cat//package])]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-read discover.cx
(ok, library, 1)"""]
    [body
      """Install takes your `[xap …]` deployment document and returns
         it with the package **pinned by hash** and its `requires`
         closure resolved beneath it — the deployment doc *is* the
         lockfile, spanning both dependency planes. In one in-memory
         program (publish a library, then install it into an empty
         deployment):"""]
    [; check=none — same --allow-random dependency as the publish
       pipeline above (the keypair); verified live 2026-07-15, output
       pinned below. ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $reg [publish geo-utils@0.1.0 as in the previous subsection]]
      [= $xap0 [xap name=my-app version='0.0.1' [features]]]
      [= $done [$xap:pkg-install $xap0 $reg 'geo-utils@0.1.0']]
  [$count $done//library]]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-random install.cx
1"""]
    [body
      """Loading pinned library code at runtime is the fourth `[?lib]`
         reference form — `pkg:` — resolved through the registry bound
         by the `CX_REGISTRY` environment variable (a store URL), with
         the full verification chain re-run on **every** load; there is
         no trust-the-registry mode. Verified live against the in-repo
         registry (run from the repository root):"""]
    [; check=none — needs a bound CX_REGISTRY plus read/env grants;
       under the grant-free docs-example gate the load fails CXER4889
       by design (the fail-closed path pinned in the table below).
       Verified live 2026-08-20 against registry/store: true. ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[?lib 'pkg:gtin@0.1.0' :as gtin]
[$gtin:valid '4006381333931']"""]
    [output
      """$ CX_REGISTRY=file://registry/store cx --allow-read --allow-env check_gtin.cx
true"""]
    [body
      """A `pkg:NAME@VERSION#MANIFEST-HASH` reference pins past the
         alias table entirely. Each failure is a value
         naming its cause:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'failure'] [th 'error']]
      [tr [td 'no registry bound (CX_REGISTRY unset)'] [td 'CXER4889 — fails closed, never a guess']]
      [tr [td 'name or version absent'] [td 'CXER4886']]
      [tr [td '#hash pin does not match the resolved manifest'] [td 'CXER4888']]
      [tr [td 'tampered tree (re-hash mismatch)'] [td 'CXER4881']]]
    [body
      """Version discipline: a version is an **alias — a name for a
         hash**; the hash is the truth. An update is a new hash and an
         explicit re-pin; a rollback is re-pinning the previous hash.
         Vendoring is first-class for offline installs: clone the
         registry store locally and verification proceeds identically
         with no network. Distinct from all of this, the older
         `cx lock` mechanism pins `https://` module fetches in a
         `cx.lock` file (the lockfile spec,
         `spec/03-approved/core/lockfile.md`) — verified live:
         `cx lock` writes bundled modules as `resolved="bundled:…"`
         entries and `cx lock --check` exits 1 on drift, printing
         expected vs actual; SRI integrity for `https://` entries is
         recorded resolved-only today (the binary says so in a
         warning), so treat `pkg:` + the pinned deployment doc as the
         XAP-era system and `cx.lock` as the module-fetch pin."""]
    [body
      """Three consequences we hold ourselves to. **Consent is grants**:
         with a runtime and a grantee named in the install options,
         install issues exactly the manifest's `needs` set as
         delegations — enabling IS granting, there is no
         installed-but-ungoverned state, a runtime request beyond
         `needs` is denied at the policy enforcement point, and a
         library, which has no `needs`, receives no grants. **An update
         is a new hash and an explicit re-pin**; one that would rebind
         existing utterances surfaces as an ambiguity, never a silent
         change, and the composer can diff two grammars — they are data
         — before you move; **a rollback is re-pinning the previous
         hash**, and the composed-grammar hash returns to its previous
         value (a downgraded feature must tolerate event kinds a newer
         version introduced — the open schema mode is the floor).
         **Yank and revocation are attestations published at the
         market**, never remote reach-in (invariant N-DIST-1): a running
         XAP is untouched and its composer surfaces the warning. The
         consuming project's `registry/pin.cx` installs every feature
         its deployment document names and writes the fully pinned
         document back (`make registry-pin` — fails loud, writes
         nothing on error), and a `tools/vendor.cx` materializes the
         required library packages from the registry, verified, for an
         offline field deployment."""]]

  [child id=dist-entitlements n=19.5 title='Entitlements and identity'
    [body
      """Commercial models ride the trust machinery rather than adding
         a DRM subsystem: **a license is a verifiable credential** — an
         attenuating delegation from the publisher to a principal — and
         the entitlement check is an ordinary policy check. Issue and
         verify, offline:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/crypto' :as crypto]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/did' :as did]
[?let [= $kp [$crypto:ed25519-keypair]]
      [= $publisher [$did:key-create $kp@public]]
      [= $vc [$xap:license-issue $publisher $kp@private 'principal:alice'
               {package: 'own-ship', versions: '0.x', kind: 'perpetual'}]]
      [= $v [$xap:license-verify $vc 'own-ship' '0.1.1' {now: '2026-07-15T00:00:00Z'}]]
  $v@status]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-random license.cx
'ok'"""]
    [body
      """Every pricing shape is an attenuation of the issued
         credential: perpetual (no expiry, a version range — a new
         major is a new purchase), subscription (short-lived VCs
         re-issued on a cadence plus a declared grace window — the
         certificate-renewal pattern, no revocation polling), per-seat
         (an org credential delegable into at most N principal-bound
         sub-credentials — the delegation chain is the count), metered
         (the consuming XAP's hash-chained journal is the
         non-repudiable meter), and trial (a time-boxed gratis VC —
         free is not a special case). The subscription grace window,
         verified — a deployment mid-passage keeps working offline past
         expiry, inside the declared grace:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/xap' :as xap]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/crypto' :as crypto]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/did' :as did]
[?let [= $kp [$crypto:ed25519-keypair]]
      [= $publisher [$did:key-create $kp@public]]
      [= $vc [$xap:license-issue $publisher $kp@private 'principal:vessel-1'
               {package: 'weather', versions: '*', kind: 'subscription',
                expires: '2026-08-01T00:00:00Z', grace-until: '2026-08-15T00:00:00Z'}]]
      [= $v [$xap:license-verify $vc 'weather' '0.1.0' {now: '2026-08-07T00:00:00Z'}]]
  $v@status]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-random grace.cx
'grace'"""]
    [body
      """**Price is a market property, never a package property** — the
         manifest carries license-terms only, so the same signed
         artifact can be paid in one market and free in another.
         `pkg-install` takes the entitlement as an option, verified
         fail-closed as part of the trust chain, and a consuming XAP
         can set `require-entitlement` as its install policy. Identity
         underneath is DID-first end to end: publishers sign as DIDs,
         principals hold DIDs, delegations are VCs — and since the
         host-auth work landed, a deployment document can carry a
         `[host-auth]` block so the deployment host itself
         authenticates attaching clients with the XSP mutual-auth
         handshake and per-request proofs (the XAP identity model spec,
         `spec/03-approved/xap/xap_identity_model.md`, and the trust-model
         part of the XAP spec)."""]]

  [child id=dist-market n=19.6 title='The marketplace — today versus specified'
    [body
      """The market model: there is **no central market**. A market is
         itself a XAP whose features are catalog, entitlement, and
         distribution; N markets compose into one discovery surface
         exactly as N XAPs compose into one experience. Honest status
         as of this release:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'piece'] [th 'status']]
      [tr [td 'packaging, signing, publish, verify, install, pkg: loading, deployment host'] [td 'implemented']]
      [tr [td 'stage-1 git registry, stage-2 served registry, catalog/discovery'] [td 'implemented — proven by the original external reference instance; the in-family successor is reference/shop']]
      [tr [td 'entitlement machinery: license-issue/verify, all pricing shapes, install policy'] [td 'implemented (engine + conformance)']]
      [tr [td 'the market as a running product (hosted catalog XAP, storefront, onboarding)'] [td 'specified, not yet implemented']]
      [tr [td 'the commerce feature + payment rails (card, invoicing, app-store, crypto)'] [td 'specified, not yet implemented (staged phase P3 of the distribution spec)']]
      [tr [td 'yank / revocation attestation flow end to end'] [td 'attestation VC machinery shipped; composer surfacing awaits the market — specified, not yet implemented']]
      [tr [td 'multi-market federation on one surface'] [td 'specified; fixture-proven as data, no runtime surface']]]
    [body
      """What that means in practice today: you can run a complete,
         trustworthy distribution system for one trust domain — publish
         sealed, signed packages to a git registry, serve it over the
         store daemon, discover with `pkg-catalog`, install by pin, and
         gate installs on entitlement credentials in any pricing shape
         (for example an internal paid tier). What you cannot do yet:
         point a customer at a hosted market, take a card payment, or
         run the yank/refund attestation flows — all specified in the
         feature distribution & market spec, staged behind its market
         and commerce phases. Revocation, when it lands, is
         **attestations published at the market, never remote
         reach-in**: a running XAP is untouched; its composer surfaces
         the warning. A catalog listing IS the feature's grammar plus
         its requirements, so discovery is a structured query, not a
         text search — design the listing as you design the
         feature."""]]

  [child id=dist-clients n=19.7 title='Clients and surfaces — the fourth project'
    [body
      """A XAP **never embeds its renderer** (invariant N-CLIENT-2, the
         clients section of the XAP spec). It exposes its surface as
         data, and every client is a separate application with its own
         spec that attaches over the wire and materializes that data
         into one medium — so the same surface drives a command line, a
         terminal, an agent and a web client with no change to the
         XAP. The **surface document** (`surfaces/<s>.surface.cxd`)
         binds already-declared feature verbs and views to media and
         layout, and never restates an intent: a panel names its
         feature, its region, and how it materializes on each medium
         present (`screen`, `audio`, `haptic` — the same intent, a
         different trigger per medium); a surface *distributes*, it
         does not render. The **client document** (`client.cxd`,
         validated by `client.cxs`) is the fourth document kind: which
         XAP it attaches to, what it consumes, how it projects, its
         windows, widgets and session model — the XAP repository's
         `reference/shop-web-client/client.cxd` is the template."""]
    [body
      """What a client consumes is the XAP's data endpoints: observe
         verbs serve as `GET /surface/<feature>` returning readouts;
         act and arrange verbs arrive as `POST /intent` — an XSP request
         frame wrapping the act, `[do 'feature/verb' [field value]…]`,
         bare or inside `[event actor= focus= …]` when the client claims
         an actor or narrows the resolution (#1260); `GET /grammar`
         serves the composed control grammar — the client's single
         control vocabulary, never a hard-coded verb list; `GET /stream`
         is the push channel, named per-feature events carrying full
         readout snapshots, so a reconnect simply re-converges. The
         routes and their fragment endpoints, replayed:"""]
    [fixture id=ux-100-surface-routes-and-fragment-endpoints]
    [body
      """**The web client is a hypermedia client.** The default
         architecture is server-rendered HTML from surface data, the
         browser a generic hypermedia client on a pinned subset of
         HTMX, JavaScript only by explicit, named exception; the client
         app — not the browser — speaks CX and XSP upstream. Run the
         pair with `make dev` (the XAP on one port, the client on the
         next, a browser), or the client alone against a running XAP
         with `cx --allow-net=127.0.0.1 --allow-read --allow-env
         serve.cx` — and `--allow-net=127.0.0.1` matters: a bare
         `--allow-net` binds, but the SSRF deny-set in the http module
         spec blocks the loopback dial to the XAP. The pinned subset:"""]
    [fixture id=web-003-the-pinned-htmx-subset]
    [body
      """**Intents ride XSP upstream.** The XAP-to-client link carries
         XSP frames (`spec/03-approved/xap/xsp.md`): a self-delimiting
         binary frame naming the principal DID, with a text or a binary
         payload; the v1 web binding is push down, `POST` up. An act
         rides as the frame's **text** payload — the CX source the host
         parses, byte for byte what it journals; a binary payload is
         object-shaped and cannot keep a child field apart from an
         attribute, so it is not the carrier for an act (#1269 tracks
         the binary carrier). The text round trip, replayed:"""]
    [fixture id=xsp-003-roundtrip-text-payload]
    [body
      """Three rules of the road. Derive the verb surface from
         `GET /grammar` and refuse unknown verbs client-side before they
         leave the browser. The client **mirrors** authority, never
         owns it — a dial toggle posts the intent and re-syncs from the
         XAP's truth, and a non-privileged attempt reverts. Agent parity
         is structural: an agent reads the identical surface data the
         human's client materializes. WebSocket and WebTransport
         bindings, multiplexing beyond the stream id, credit
         backpressure and reconnect-resume are scoped in the XSP spec
         and not yet implemented."""]]

  [child id=dist-reference-surfaces n=19.8 title='The reference surfaces — ORIEL and the studio'
    [body
      """Two worked surfaces show the declarative way end to end, and
         both live with the repositories that own them rather than on
         this page. **ORIEL** is the reference storefront — built on three shipped
         modules, `ux`, `ux-web` and `ux-tui` — a catalogue of products with
         departments and facets, a session-scoped basket, a four-step
         checkout, subscriptions, returns and reviews, in a browser, in
         a terminal and in a serial voice-style renderer, from
         declarations, with zero view code. Its four documents are the
         domain (a feature document), the composition (a surface
         document), the theme (every appearance decision, and the only
         place one may live) and the service (folds, the command layer,
         page composition — no view code); what polices the arrangement
         is not discipline but six instruments that walk the running
         store and refuse loudly when a declaration and a rendering
         disagree. **The studio** is the design tool for a live XAP: a
         designer opens a page of a running app, selects a block, drags
         it, resizes it, rewrites its words, restyles the brand — and
         every act is a journaled command against a base document, so
         shipping an improvement to every deployment that customized
         the app is a replay, not a merge. The studio edits
         arrangements, and an arrangement is projected by a face; the
         same document renders on all three faces, which is why a
         studio edit cannot mean "add some CSS" — a value the terminal
         cannot honour is not admissible."""]
    [body
      """Both are the ux repository's — its own step runs ORIEL's
         drive, keys, voice, nokernel and diff instruments on every
         commit — and the studio's clauses are the ux capability spec's
         (`spec/03-approved/xap/ux.md` §18–§19); the in-family reference
         deployment beside them is the XAP repository's
         `reference/shop` with `reference/shop-web-client`. The pages
         that own them on this site: the ux repository's page
         (`repo-cx-platform-ux.html`), the XAP repository's page
         (`repo-cx-platform-xap.html`), and the ux capability's own
         section, [[ux-capability]]. The content normal form every
         face consumes, replayed:"""]
    [fixture id=ux-030-content-normal-form]]
]
